'''Suldalsvatnet'''<ref name="name">{{Cite web |title=Informasjon om stadnamn |url=https://stadnamn.kartverket.no/fakta/791668 |access-date=2026-03-15 |website=Norgeskart |publisher=[[Norwegian Mapping Authority|Kartverket]] |language=no}}</ref> ({{lit|Lake Suldal}}<ref>{{Cite book |last=Wilson |first=Damon |title=The World's Greatest Unsolved Mysteries |publisher=Barnes and Noble Books |year=2004 |location=New York |page=271}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Fodor |first=Eugene |title=Fodor's Scandinavia 1977 |publisher=David McKay Co. |year=1977 |location=New York |page=272}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Nordland |first=Odd |title=Brewing and Beer Traditions in Norway: The Social Anthropological Background of the Brewing Industry |publisher=Universitetsforlaget |year=1969 |location=Oslo |page=141}}</ref>) is the sixth-deepest [[List of lakes of Norway|lake in Norway]].  The {{convert|376|m|adj=on}} deep lake lies in [[Suldal Municipality]] in [[Rogaland]] county, [[Norway]]. The {{convert|29|km2|adj=on}} lake is the headwaters of the river [[Suldalslågen]] and it sits at an elevation of {{convert|69|m}} above sea level. The lake has a volume of {{convert|4.51|km3}}.<ref name="snl">{{Cite SNL|Suldalsvatnet|Suldalsvatnet|2026-03-15|Thorsnæs|Geir|2024-11-25|editor2-first=Per Roger|editor2-last=Lauritzen}}</ref>

The {{convert|29|km|adj=on}} long lake is regulated for use in two nearby [[hydroelectric power plant]]s.<ref name="snl" />

